=== cold_open
@act COLD OPEN
@pov cal
@slug SOUTH STATION, BOSTON — 6:50 A.M.

Deck dropped him at six in his uncle Jimmy's truck, got out to help with the suitcase, and didn't help with it. He watched Cal fight the broken wheel, then shoved him in the chest the way he'd been shoving him since first communion and laughed the big red laugh that had gotten them thrown out of CCD and two wakes. "Don't come back talking like them. I'm serious. You come home saying 'genuinely,' I'm putting you through the Dunks window. I'm just saying."

He got back in the truck. The window came down.

"Cal." Quieter. "We're good, Cal. We're good."

"I know," Cal said, to the taillights.

His mother hadn't come. She'd worked a double at the Quiet Man, and on the fridge at Gold Street she'd left two twenties under the Sully's magnet and a note.

[note from="Ma"]Cally. Knock em dead. Don't let them see you eat, they can smell it. There's a sandwich in the fridge, don't eat it, it's from Tuesday, I'm just saying. Call your mother, she's a fun lady. Love, Ma. P.S. Forty bucks under the Sully's magnet, that's NOT for the twins, they have me. xo[/note]

Not for the twins, which meant it was for the twins. The twenties were in his sock now with the rest. Two hundred and twelve dollars, flat against the ankle bone.

On the 6:50 Peter Pan he had Deck's mother's plaid suitcase underneath, his duffel at his feet, and his Discman shut with a rubber band.

[needle] The Dirty Glass | Dropkick Murphys | Cal's Discman on the Peter Pan

A man and a woman screaming at each other across a bar, which was as close as Cal's collection came to a love song. Across the aisle a heavy man in a Sox cap read the *Herald* and breathed through his mouth. The Pike, then 91 north, Massachusetts thinning out into farm stands.

The Calder Falls depot, around nine-fifteen, was a bank of pay phones and a TV over the ticket counter showing a storm in the Gulf, the white cone laid across the map like a finger. Nobody watched it. The man in the Sox cap got off too.

Main Street ran a mile uphill and the wheel clacked the whole way: the Hollis Bookstore, a diner, St. Anne's with its doors propped for the heat, a dead brick mill with the river loud under it. At the top, iron gates, 1791 in the arch. Behind him as he went through came the dry ratchet of a disposable camera. The man in the Sox cap was lowering a yellow Kodak, squinting at the gates. Tourists.

[sms from="Deck" time="9:41 AM"]u there yet harvard. dont let them tie a sweater on u. they do it while ur sleeping[/sms]

[sms to="Deck" time="9:43 AM"]not harvard. 2 sweaters so far. both on dads[/sms]

Sutter Hall's curb was a traffic jam of money. A black Range Rover with the tailgate up, a Saab 9-3 behind it, a woman in a silk scarf directing two boys and a hand truck with the calm of someone who had moved many households and carried none of them. A tall man in a blue oxford stood at the tailgate on his cell. He didn't lower it. He looked at Cal, at the plaid suitcase, and pointed at a box. His other hand held out a twenty, folded lengthwise, the way you'd tip a valet.

His eyes went to the scar through Cal's eyebrow, seven weeks old and still pink, and away.

* Carry the box. Say nothing. -> curb_after | set: s1e1_curb="carried"
* "I'm the roommate, sir. I carry things for free. I'm told it's in the brochure." -> curb_after | set: s1e1_curb="corrected"
