=== kit_reply

{if s1e1_kit_opening == "charm"}
"Enemies is a big commitment. We haven't even had coffee."

"Respectfully, Reardon, this isn't a courtship. It's a deposition, and you're the exhibit."

"You looked me up and checked out my shoes. Where I'm from that's a second date."

"Noted," Brennan said, bone dry, but he saw the corner of her mouth go, and saw her furious at it.
{/if}
{if s1e1_kit_opening == "cut"}
"You memorized the Register. On move-in day."

"It's called preparation."

"It's Saturday night and you're in a wool blazer in a church, reciting strangers' hometowns. Who's that for?"

"For the record, that was a cheap shot." She smiled, slow and pleased, like a fencer who'd found somebody already holding a foil. "Do it again."
{/if}
{if s1e1_kit_opening == "agree"}
"Good," Cal said. "I've never had a real one."

Kit had three sentences lined up behind her teeth. None of them fit. "You're supposed to argue."

"Why? You made a good case."

She turned back to the book as if it had insulted her mother.
{/if}

She signed *Katherine Anne Brennan* in a quick hard slant. Cal took the pen, a nib that punished left-handers, and signed *Callum Patrick Reardon* on the same page, three lines below *Delphine E. Ashcombe* in a careful upright hand.

Henry peered around his shoulder. "She signed like she was copying it."

"Who?"

"She came in at four, in black pants and a white shirt. She didn't read the plaque." He took out his notebook. "Everybody reads the plaque."

{if s1e1_kit_opening == "charm"}
Brennan's slant was still drying three lines up. He found he'd have liked to see that mouth-corner go again.
{/if}
{if s1e1_kit_opening == "cut"}
Brennan was already at the door with her chin up, and the strange part was how much better he felt for having been hit by someone good.
{/if}
{if s1e1_kit_opening == "agree"}
Brennan was waiting by the door, not looking at him, which was a way of looking. He'd have rather been arguing.
{/if}

Delphine, Cal thought. It sounded like a boat. He handed Henry the pen and went out into the long gold heat, wiping ink off the side of his hand.

